2. How CrewAI works

CrewAI splits a project into three kinds of objects. An agent is a role-playing worker defined by a role, goal, and backstory, plus the LLM it uses. A task says what work gets done: a description, an expected_output, and the agent that owns it. A crew assembles the agents and tasks and runs them with a Process. Here I used Process.sequential, so research_task runs first, its output is handed to writing_task, and that output goes to editing_task.

The role, goal, and backstory are put into the prompt for every LLM call that agent makes. That is why my edit worked. The writer’s task text (“write a 250-word explainer on {topic}”) did not change, but the persona wrapped around it did. The {topic} placeholder is filled by crew.kickoff(inputs={"topic": topic}). The research brief reaches the writer automatically through the sequential process. The CFO persona therefore only reshaped how the writer turned the same kind of brief into prose. The editor then received a draft that was already cost-framed and applied its own job, tightening and adding [source needed] flags.

